{"lines": ["Back in Kentucky, the first newspaper to report the Hopkinsville story said there were twelve to fifteen creatures.", "The word green was not in the early articles. The colour was added later.", "The Air Force's Project Blue Book later listed the case as a hoax, with no comment.", "It still became a teaching example. Two psychologists, Schmaltz and Lilienfeld, used it in a 2014 paper in Frontiers in Psychology as a teaching case in pseudoscience. It also inspired Steven Spielberg's unproduced film Night Skies, and the 1986 film Critters.", "And Mothman got a second life. On the fifteenth of December, 1967, the Silver Bridge in Point Pleasant collapsed. Forty-six people died.", "Later, writers tied the monster to the disaster. Gray Barker's 1970 book The Silver Bridge was heavily fictionalized, and John Keel's 1975 book The Mothman Prophecies carried the link on.", "Another skeptic, Reed, wrote in Skeptical Inquirer in 2022 that a great blue heron was statistically likelier.", "Rule five. A real tragedy can attach itself to a story that came before it. Ask when each part was first written down."], "words": [["Back", 0.0, 0.18], ["in", 0.18, 0.42], ["Kentucky,", 0.42, 0.78], ["the", 1.3, 1.35], ["first", 1.35, 1.6], ["newspaper", 1.6, 1.92], ["to", 1.92, 2.5], ["report", 2.5, 2.72], ["the", 2.72, 3.08], ["Hopkinsville", 3.08, 3.82], ["story", 3.82, 4.12], ["said", 4.12, 4.6], ["there", 4.6, 4.82], ["were", 4.82, 4.94], ["twelve", 4.94, 5.32], ["to", 5.32, 5.54], ["fifteen", 5.54, 5.94], ["creatures.", 5.94, 6.24], ["The", 7.36, 7.46], ["word", 7.46, 7.64], ["green", 7.64, 7.94], ["was", 7.94, 8.3], ["not", 8.3, 8.58], ["in", 8.58, 8.8], ["the", 8.8, 8.9], ["early", 8.9, 9.18], ["articles.", 9.18, 9.68], ["The", 10.14, 10.26], ["colour", 10.26, 10.48], ["was", 10.48, 10.74], ["added", 10.74, 11.04], ["later.", 11.04, 11.46], ["The", 12.26, 12.38], ["Air", 12.38, 12.56], ["Force's", 12.56, 13.08], ["Project", 13.08, 13.64], ["Blue", 13.64, 13.92], ["Book", 1